eng W6, Play W2, Play T2, Play B9, Play B1.\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play T8, Play T5, Play T3, Play T3, Play J2, Play F2, Play T9, Peng B2, Play W2, Chi B8, Play W9, Play W8, Play W9, Play W8, Play F1, Chi W3, Play B5, Play F3, Play B4, Play T4.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play T8; Play B5; Play B8; Play W4; Play T7; Play F4; Play W3; Play J2; Play B7]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation:  \n- **Play T8**: This tile is relatively safe as T8 has not been frequently discarded by others, but it's not in a suit where much meld activity has occurred. However, keeping it might help form a potential chow with T7 and possibly T6/T9, though T6 and T9 are risky.\n- **Play B5**: B5 is part of an existing meld (B3-B4-B5), but playing it now would discard the middle tile of that sequence. Since B3 and B4 have been used in a completed meld, B5 is expendable. However, opponents may be using fives; unshown count shows only one B5 left.\n- **Play B8**: We hold two B8s, and one B8 was used in the previous player\u2019s chow [B7, B8, B9], so third B8 remains unseen. Playing one B8 could be dangerous if someone needs it for a pair or kong, but we still retain one copy.\n- **Play W4**: W4 appears in our hand and also in the across player's meld [W3,W4,W5] and previous player's [W2,W3,W4]. Two copies remain unseen. Risky to discard.\n- **Play T7**: T7 is held singly. Across player has T3s, next has T1Kong, no visible T7 activity. Unseen: 3 copies remain. Relatively safe.\n- **Play F4**: Only one F4 in hand. Prevailing wind is North (F3), seat wind East (F1). F4 is not a significant wind tile. One F4 already discarded by next player. Two unseen \u2014 moderate risk.\n- **Play W3**: W3 is already exposed in multiple players\u2019 melds ([W3,W4,W5] and [W2,W3,W4]). Only one W3 left unseen. Discarding it may complete someone\u2019s pung/kong \u2014 very dangerous.\n- **Play J2**: We have two J2s. No J2 has been seen in any melds or discards. Three copies remain unseen. Green Dragon is less commonly used, but still possible. Holding two gives flexibility for pair.\n- **Play B7**: B7 is in our hand and was used in previous player\u2019s meld [B7,B8,B9]. They may be waiting on another B7 for a pung or pair. One B7 discarded earlier. Three unseen \u2014 moderate danger.\n\nReason:  \nWe must prioritize safety and avoid feeding opponents' likely hands. W3 is extremely dangerous due to multiple exposures. B8 is slightly risky but we keep one copy. T7 stands out as relatively safe \u2014 no player has shown interest in T7, T8, or T9 beyond general play, and there\u2019s no indication of a terminal flush or straight involving them. Also, our hand lacks strong direction; we have fragments across suits and honors. T7 does not contribute to any clear progression (no T6/T9 nearby anymore), and we\u2019ve previously discarded T6, T5, T9, etc., suggesting we're breaking down that suit. Keeping T8 over T7 offers better internal coordination (T7+T8), so discarding T7 breaks less synergy.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
